texprobe: a guest program for comparing texturing on a real Radeon and in
the emulator, texel by texel.

texprobe.c   Mac OS X 10.4 (PowerPC), GLUT.  Uploads textures in which
             every texel names its level, face and place, draws each level
             at one texel per pixel (GL_NEAREST_MIPMAP_NEAREST, so the card
             picks the level) and reads the pixels back.  One line per
             level in /tmp/texprobe.txt, PASS or FAIL with the first wrong
             texels.  Then replaces part of two levels with
             glTexSubImage2D and checks again ("sub").  The last lines:
             TOTAL (texture levels), CONTROLS (stage 3 source patterns
             that did not read back as drawn, framebuffer objects that
             were not complete, GL errors) and RESULT.
             Exit status 1 when a level failed, a control failed, or
             nothing was run (only= naming no case).  A level line
             checked after a copy whose source pattern was wrong says
             SOURCE-INVALID: it says nothing about the copy.
               gcc -std=gnu99 -O2 -o texprobe texprobe.c \
                   -framework GLUT -framework OpenGL
               ./texprobe [stage=1|2|3|4|5] [only=ID] [hold=SECONDS] [wait=SECONDS]
                          [src=pixels] [fbo=none]
             stage 1: 32-bit, 16-bit and DXT textures, among them chains
             with one-row levels many microtiles wide (1024x4), levels
             wider than a macrotile but not as tall (2048x8), and cubes.
             stage 2: 8-bit textures and two-byte luminance-alpha, and
             textures of every kind without mip levels.
             stage 3: copies from the frame buffer (glCopyTexSubImage2D,
             as Doom 3's heat haze) into an RGBA8 and a 1555 texture,
             32x16 with mip levels: a pattern of known colours is drawn
             (a quad textured one texel a pixel, or with src=pixels by
             glDrawPixels) and read back ("SRC" lines), then copied
             into part of level 0, the whole of it, two overlapping
             parts, overwritten in part by glTexSubImage2D, and copied
             into part of level 1.  Every level is checked after each
             step, so the texels outside a rectangle and the other
             levels are checked as well.  tags.py does not know what
             these textures hold.  With src=pixels it first draws three
             patterns cut by the window's right, left and bottom edges
             (CLIP lines, controls): the host-data blit's clipping.
             stage 4: drawing into textures through a framebuffer object
             (GL_EXT_framebuffer_object): an RGBA8 and a 1555 texture with
             mip levels, level 0 attached, three overlapping rectangles --
             added (glBlendFunc ONE, ONE), written through a colour mask
             (red and blue), and both (green and alpha added) -- then every
             level checked against what the probe computes.  A framebuffer
             object that is not complete is a failed control: nothing was
             drawn, and the levels would pass unchanged.  fbo=none attaches
             nothing, to show that such a run fails.
             stage 5: all of these into one texture, where the conventions
             meet: an RGBA8 and a 1555 texture with mip levels, uploaded,
             copied into in part, drawn into through a framebuffer object
             over the copy and the upload (added; written through a colour
             mask), then replaced in part by glTexSubImage2D over all three.
             Every level is checked after each step, and read back whole
             with glGetTexImage first, after the copy, after the drawing
             and last (GET lines, counted as levels): a read of the
             texture's memory by the driver, not a draw.
             stage 6: textures made by glCopyTexImage2D instead of uploaded,
             the kind the driver reads back through a swap-0 surface: an
             RGBA8 and a 1555 (GL_RGB5_A1) texture, one level each, with
             the format the driver reports it chose (MAKE lines); then a
             copy into part, a framebuffer object's drawing and a
             glTexSubImage2D, each overlapping the last.  After each step
             the level is read back with glGetTexImage and drawn from.
             When a level reads back as another level's texels the draw is
             repeated with a LOD bias; the line then says "bias +N".  Such
             a PASS says the level's texels are right, not that the level
             was chosen rightly.
tags.py      Finds texprobe's levels in a capture of the emulator's VRAM
             (R300_CAP=prefix R300_CAP_TEX=1, then "echo 9000 > prefix.go"
             before texprobe starts) and compares the places with the
             layout the emulator's sampler uses.

What they say, and what they do not:
- texprobe.txt is a test of what comes out.  On hardware a clean run shows
  the driver, the card and the probe agree.  In the emulator a FAIL on a
  level that passes on hardware is an emulator bug, provided the probe's
  own assumptions hold there (valid GL, the formats the driver chose).
  Neither run says anything about the order of texels inside a level in a
  card's memory: an upload and a sampler that both go through the same
  address translation give the same picture as none at all.
- tags.py reads captured emulator memory: where the emulator stored the
  texels while executing the driver's commands, untiled.  It is not a view
  of a real card's memory, and it does not by itself say which place the
  driver meant: a small level's row is usually found several times (all
  listed), and "ok" only means the sampler's offset is one of them.
- The driver's commands are the better witness for placement: with
  PPCGPU_SEQ_LOG=1 the emulator logs each blit (/tmp/gpu_seq.log), and in
  the first uploads captured a level is one blit to that level's offset
  and pitch.  A blit says where and how much; what filled the rows it
  adds is inferred from the logged source offsets and the bytes found.
- tags.py exits 1 when a level differs and 2 when nothing differs but a
  case of the stage was not captured: no data is not a pass.
- The capture is best effort: a texture is dumped again when a hash of its
  bytes changes, so a state that returns, or a collision, is not recorded.

Results, 2026-10-12 (Tiger 10.4.11, ATI-1.4.18):
- PowerBook G4 (ATY,RV360M11): stage 1 486 of 486 levels PASS, stage 2
  182 of 182, all at bias +0.
- Emulator (Radeon 9700, Metal), with the 8-bit microtile rule: stage 1
  464 of 486, every first upload PASS; stage 2 180 of 182, every first
  upload PASS.  All FAILs follow glTexSubImage2D: 32-bit textures sample
  with their components in other places (whole texture); 16-bit textures
  have the replaced texels byte-swapped; a replaced part of a small 8-bit
  level (8 texels wide) does not reach memory at all.  Not fixed.
  In one of four stage 2 runs the replaced part of the A8 1024x4 base
  level also read back old texels though memory held the new ones.
- Before the 8-bit rule stage 2 failed 93 of 161 levels (8-bit textures
  with rows under 32 bytes).
- Driver's blits (emulator, PPCGPU_SEQ_LOG): A8 1024x4 levels 1.. go to
  +0x1000 (512x4, pitch 512), +0x1800 (256x4), +0x1c00, +0x1e00, +0x1f00,
  +0x1f80 (16x4, pitch 16), +0x1fc0 (8x4, pitch 8), +0x1fe0, +0x2000,
  +0x2020; ARGB8888 1024x4 to +0x4000 (512x2, pitch 2048), +0x5000 (256x2),
  +0x5800, +0x5c00, +0x5e00, +0x5f00, +0x5f80, +0x5fc0 (4x2, pitch 16),
  +0x5fe0, +0x6000.
- glTexSubImage2D (same log): no blit; a 3D draw with the level as its
  colour buffer and the new texels as a texture in AGP memory, e.g.
  "R3D rt=3e0000/4096 tex0=103904a0 5x3" for the base level and
  "R3D rt=3e1fc0/32 tex0=10390980 3x1" for the 8x1 level of the A8
  chain, "rt=533f80/32 ... 3x1" for that of the ARGB8888 one (that log
  gave pixels times four whatever the format: the A8 level's rows are
  8 bytes, not 32; it now prints both): the
  height-rounded offsets again.  So the emulator's failures after an
  update are failures of drawing into a texture.  Read from the code,
  not yet tested by a fix: the emulator stores what the card draws in
  the card's byte order and remembers the address as such a surface, so
  a 32-bit texture is then read that way as a whole and a 16-bit one
  holds the drawn texels in the other order; and the Metal renderer
  returns without drawing when a colour buffer's rows are not a multiple
  of its linear-texture alignment (16 bytes), which an 8-texel 8-bit
  level's are not.  Measured: on Vulkan stage 2 passes 182 of 182, and
  stage 1 fails the same 22 levels as on Metal.

Results, 2026-10-13 (same machines; emulator at vs-unread 27a0bc5):
- Stage 2 on Metal: 182 of 182 since 3bd899e (an 8-texel 8-bit level's
  update is drawn in the private staging and copied back).
- Stage 1 with R300_RT_LE=0: 484 of 486.  The twenty 32-bit failures come
  from the emulator's record of surfaces drawn by the 3D engine, which
  switches the whole texture to the drawn byte order after a partial
  update; the two left are 16-bit (T09): the replaced texels hold the
  right values in the opposite byte order to their neighbours.
- Stage 3 on the PowerBook: 72 of 72, the pattern drawn either way.
- Stage 3 in the emulator, Metal and Vulkan: the copies are 3D draws
  into the texture level (format 6 or 3, endian 0) from the GL back
  buffer as a texture (endian 0, macro-tiled).  RGBA8: the copied texels
  are stored in the host's order (B G R A), the uploaded ones and those
  of glTexSubImage2D in the CPU's (A R G B); with the record on, every
  texel but the copied ones then reads wrong, with R300_RT_LE=0 only the
  copied ones.  1555: every texel the GPU wrote, copied or replaced, is
  byte-swapped.  On Metal the partial copy sometimes read back the old
  texels although memory held the new ones (3 of 6 runs, the 1555 level
  and once the RGBA8 one; Vulkan never): the display's dirty-log clear
  covers whole 256 KB blocks, and the textures lay in the block the frame
  ends in, so the write count that vouches for a cached copy missed the
  draw.  With the frame noted as the blocks it clears (81bc7e9): 0 of 4.  The
  stage 2 stale read of 2026-10-12 (A8 1024x4 at 0x3e1000) was in the
  same block; likely the same cause, not run often enough since to show.
- With src=pixels the emulator drew nothing: glDrawPixels arrives as a
  PM4 type-3 packet 0x94 (CNTL_HOSTDATA_BLT in the radeon headers), which
  the device did not handle.  Since fdd7a91 all 10 patterns read back,
  and every level line is the same as with the quad, checksums included.
  The three CLIP cases read back whole there and on the PowerBook; the
  driver sends the whole rectangle (X -3, rows past the bottom) and lets
  the clip registers cut it.
- Stage 4: PowerBook 24 of 24; emulator 24 of 24 on Metal and Vulkan.
  The driver draws these textures with COLORENDIAN 2 (32-bit; 1 for
  1555) and US_OUT_FMT 1b01, where glCopyTexSubImage2D into the same kind
  of texture uses COLORENDIAN 0 with the same selectors; both read back
  right on the PowerBook.

Results, 2026-10-14 (same machines; emulator at vs-unread with the
byte-order model's aperture 0 conversion, R300_ENDIAN_MODEL=1):
- PowerBook: stage 4 24 of 24 and stage 5 72 of 72 (quad and
  src=pixels); with fbo=none every level passes and the run fails
  (framebuffer objects 2, incomplete 2; exit status 1).  The emulator
  gives the same with fbo=none.
- Emulator, R300_ENDIAN_MODEL=1, Metal and Vulkan: stage 1 486 of 486,
  stage 2 182 of 182, stage 3 72 of 72 (quad and src=pixels), stage 4
  24 of 24, stage 5 72 of 72.  Without the model: stage 1 464, stage 3
  38, stage 4 24, stage 5 54 (the copies, as in stage 3).
- The driver reads the 1555 texture back (glGetTexImage) through
  aperture 0 with surfaces set to swap 1, one per level.  Converting
  those as for 32-bit data gave neighbouring texels exchanged (lv0 0 of
  512); left straight they read right, the model's 16-bit rule matching
  that view.  The swap-0 surfaces the driver sets in stage 5 saw no CPU
  reads at all, so this probe does not exercise the conversion itself;
  rbprobe's glCopyTexImage2D + glGetTexImage does (4096 reads converted,
  4096 of 4096 texels right; 0 of 4096 with R300_AP0_SURFACES=0).

Later the same day, stage 5 with glGetTexImage after the copy and after
the drawing as well (96 levels), the emulator with its aperture 0
surfaces looked up after the catch-up and SURFACEn registers readable:
- PowerBook: stage 5 96 of 96 (quad and src=pixels); fbo=none fails
  (exit status 1).
- Emulator, R300_ENDIAN_MODEL=1: stage 5 96 of 96 on Metal (quad and
  src=pixels) and Vulkan, and 96 of 96 with R300_AP0_SURFACES=0 too:
  stage 5 does not tell the two apart.  $R300_SURFLOG shows why: the
  driver reads the RGBA8 levels back through surfaces set to swap 2
  (SURFACE0_INFO 0x00a3....), which need no conversion, and the 1555
  levels through swap 1.  Its swap-0 surfaces here are passing states,
  INFO written before the bounds.  rbprobe's texture, made by
  glCopyTexImage2D, is read through swap 0 (INFO 0x00030020).
  Stages 1-4 as before: 486, 182, 72 (and 72 src=pixels), 24 on Metal
  and Vulkan.  Without the model: stage 1 464, stage 3 38, stage 4 24,
  stage 5 74 of 96.

Stage 6, 2026-10-14 (textures made by glCopyTexImage2D; emulator at
surf-save, R300_ENDIAN_MODEL=1 unless said):
- PowerBook: 16 of 16 (quad and src=pixels); fbo=none fails (exit
  status 1).  For GL_RGB5_A1 the driver reports 8057, bits 5 5 5 1: it
  keeps a 16-bit texture.
- Emulator: 16 of 16 on Metal (quad and src=pixels) and Vulkan; fbo=none
  fails as on the PowerBook.  With R300_AP0_SURFACES=0: 12 of 16, the
  four RGBA8 GET lines 0 of 512 with each texel's bytes reversed (got
  85b59418 want 1894b585) and every draw right -- this stage tells the
  two apart, as rbprobe does.  Without the model: 6 of 16 (the 1555
  texture wrong throughout; the RGBA8 one's glTexSubImage2D texels
  reversed).
- $R300_SURFLOG: the driver reads the RGBA8 texture back through a swap-0
  surface (512 reads converted for each GET) and the 1555 one through
  swap 1, left straight; the swap-0 settings the 1555 one passes through
  get no reads.  16-bit data under swap 0 was not seen here either: that
  case remains unexercised.
- $R300_DRAWLOG: both are sampled macro- and micro-tiled with endian 0
  (TX_OFFSET 0x003e100c, 0x003e200c); the framebuffer object draws into
  the RGBA8 one with COLORENDIAN 0 (RB3D_COLORPITCH0 0x00c30020), where
  stage 4's uploaded textures were drawn into with 2.
